## Inheritance
Sometimes classes share common behavior. A `Dog` and `Cat` are both `Animal`s. Instead of duplicating code, use inheritance:

```ts
class Animal {
    name: string;
    sound: string;

    constructor(name: string, sound: string) {
        this.name = name;
        this.sound = sound;
    }

    speak(): string {
        return this.name + " says " + this.sound;
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    constructor(name: string) {
        super(name, "Woof");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    constructor(name: string) {
        super(name, "Meow");
    }
}

const dog = new Dog("Rex");
console.log(dog.speak());  // Rex says Woof
const cat = new Cat("Whiskers");
console.log(cat.speak());  // Whiskers says Meow
```

- `extends` makes a class inherit from another
- `super()` calls the parent's constructor
- Child classes get ALL parent methods and fields
- Child classes can add their own methods and fields

## Overriding Methods
A child can replace a parent's method:

```ts
class LoudDog extends Animal {
    constructor(name: string) {
        super(name, "WOOF");
    }

    speak(): string {
        return this.name + " SCREAMS " + this.sound + "!!!";
    }
}

const loud = new LoudDog("Bruno");
console.log(loud.speak());  // Bruno SCREAMS WOOF!!!
```

